Imagine you've spent six hours building a complex A I agent workflow. It's deep in a debugging session, methodically working through a gnarly problem. Then your laptop battery dies. When you reboot and reconnect, the session is gone, the agent's context is wiped, and you're starting from scratch. This happens constantly in A I engineering circles, and the solution has been sitting in Linux servers for fifteen years, largely ignored.

The tool is called T M A X, short for Terminal Multiplexer, and it's becoming essential infrastructure for anyone serious about agentic development.

At its core, T M A X solves one fundamental problem: terminal sessions that survive disconnection. When you run a command in a normal terminal window and close your laptop, that process dies. T M A X creates persistent containers called sessions that keep running regardless of what happens to your local machine. Your laptop can lose power, you can switch devices mid-task, you can close the lid and walk away. The agent keeps working.

The terminology takes five minutes to learn. Sessions are the top-level containers, named so you can reference them later. Windows are like browser tabs, multiple running inside a single session. Panes are split screens within those windows, and here's the clever part: agents can read and interact with other panes in the same session. You're not just running parallel terminals; you're building a workspace where your tools can see each other.

The commands are straightforward. Create a session with "tee mux new" and a name. Detach with control B then D, which drops you back to your normal terminal while the session stays alive. Reattach with "tee mux attach" targeting the session name. List everything running with "tee mux L S". If things go sideways, "tee mux kill dash server" wipes everything clean.

The real power emerges when you pair T M A X with a virtual private server. Cheap V P S providers like HostingHer offer K V M two plans that cost less than a monthly streaming subscription. Install T M A X with apt, enable mouse support for easier navigation, then S S H in from anywhere. Your agents run on that remote machine instead of draining your local battery. You can check progress from your phone, a tablet, or a borrowed computer. Multiple agents run simultaneously on the same project or completely different ones.

This setup enables workflows that would otherwise require expensive cloud infrastructure or constant babysitting. Slash goal commands that execute for twenty-four hours or more. Full-stack web applications built entirely through A I agents while you sleep. A single developer can orchestrate multiple specialized agents working in parallel, each in its own pane, each contributing to a larger workflow.

The takeaway is practical rather than philosophical: the people building autonomous A I systems still need good tooling to manage them. T M A X on a V P S isn't glamorous, but it's the kind of infrastructure that quietly makes everything else possible.